te a small days-from-civil function (Howard Hinnant's algorithm) to compute epoch days and validate ranges by checking that re-deriving the calendar date from the days gives back the same date (or simply validate month/day against the month-length table including leap years). Actually the simplest and most robust is:\n\n1. Parse the fields with the regex: y (4 digits), mo, d, hh, mm, ss.\n2. Validate: 1 <= mo <= 12; 1 <= d <= daysInMonth(y, mo); 0 <= hh <= 23; 0 <= mm <= 59; 0 <= ss <= 59. (Leap seconds 60? \"A real calendar moment\" — 60 seconds is a leap second and is not representable; treat as invalid. Keep ss <= 59.)\n3. Convert to epoch ms via daysFromCivil * 86400000 + (hh*3600 + mm*60 + ss) * 1000.\n\ndaysInMonth with leap year: leap = (y % 4 === 0 && y % 100 !== 0) || y % 400 === 0.\n\ndaysFromCivil (Hinnant):\n```\nfunction daysFromCivil(y, m, d) {\n  let yy = y;\n  if (m <= 2) yy--;\n  const era = Math.floor(yy / 400); // careful with negative years; our years are >= 0 so fine\n  const yoe = yy - era * 400;          // [0, 399]\n  const mp = (m + 9) % 12;             // month 0=March\n  const doy = Math.floor((153 * mp + 2) / 5) + d - 1; // [0, 365]\n  const doe = yoe * 365 + Math.floor(yoe / 4) - Math.floor(yoe / 100) + doy;\n  return era * 146097 + doe - 719468;\n}\n```\nYears are always >= 0 (4 digits), so no issue with negative era.\n\nThen epochMs = days * 86400000 + secs * 1000. For comparison purposes, as long as it's consistent and monotonic, that's enough. Range: year 9999 → days ~2.9e6 → ms ~2.5e14, safely within double-precision integers.\n\nOverlap: aStartMs < bEndMs && bStartMs < aEndMs.\n\nThen results:\n```\nconst pairs = [];\nfor i < j: same room && overlap → const [x, y] = idA < idB ? [idA, idB] : [idB, idA]; pairs.push([x, y]);\npairs.sort((p, q) => (p[0] < q[0] ? -1 : p[0] > q[0] ? 1 : p[1] < q[1] ? -1 : p[1] > q[1] ? 1 : 0));\n```\n\nWait — R5 says \"pairs are sorted by idA, then by idB\". Since ids are unique, can a pair appear twice?